# Trustworthy checks

## A check that cannot fail is worse than no check

This is the asymmetry that decides everything else. A check which always passes
and a check which is absent produce the **same observable outcome**: no findings.
One of them is reported as evidence and the other is not, so the broken one is
strictly more dangerous. It converts "I did not look" into "I looked and there was
nothing", and it does so silently, indefinitely, for as long as nobody plants a
defect to test it.

So the first question about any check is not "is it correct" but **"what would it
do if the thing it watches for were present?"** If you cannot answer that, you
do not have a check; you have a ritual.

## Prove it by planting the defect

The only test of a detector is a known-positive. A test that runs the check
against a clean input and asserts zero findings proves nothing, because that is
also the behaviour of a check that is not looking at all.

```sh
# worthless: passes whether or not the detector works
assert_check_reports_nothing_on_clean_input

# worth something: the detector must find the thing it exists to find
plant_known_defect_in_a_temp_copy
assert_check_reports_it
```

Plant it in a fixture, not in the repository, and remove it afterwards — including
when the assertion fails, or the next run inherits your defect and passes for the
wrong reason.

**Pin both directions.** A detector that flags everything and one that flags
nothing fail in opposite ways and are equally useless. The loud one is usually
diagnosed faster, which is exactly why it is safer: it produces an obvious wrong
answer that gets investigated. Test both, because each mistake is invisible in the
other's test.

## "Clean" is a claim about a population

A check that reports no findings has not reported success. It has reported that
nothing in **the population it actually examined** matched. Those differ whenever
the search is narrower than intended:

- a glob that matched nothing, so there was nothing to look at
- a path that does not exist, so the tool errored and the error was swallowed
- an empty input list, so the loop body never ran
- a filter that rejected every candidate before the check ever saw one

**Report what you scanned, not only what you found.** A count in the output turns
"clean" into evidence, because it distinguishes "examined 996 files, found
nothing" from "examined nothing". This is cheap and it is the single highest-value
line of output most checkers omit.

```
everything is already normalized (996 files scanned)
```

If the count is zero, the check has told you nothing and should say so loudly.

## Check the exit status the caller actually sees

The result of a check is its **exit status**, not its output. Everything between
the two is where checks quietly stop working:

| Trap | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| `if ! cmd; then ...` with output consumed | status survives, fine |
| `cmd \| tee log` | status is `tee`'s, always zero |
| `cmd; echo $?` after an intervening command | reports the wrong command |
| `local out=$(cmd)` then `[ -n "$out" ]` | a failure with output passes |
| `set -e` inside a `\|` pipeline | the left side is not checked |
| `find ... \| xargs check` | `xargs` returns 123 when any check failed |

```sh
# wrong: green regardless of what the checker did
find . -name '*.md' | xargs -0 ./check.sh | tee /tmp/report

# right: propagate 123 and check every form
find . -print0 | xargs -0 -r ./check.sh; rc=$?
case $rc in 0) ;; 123|1) exit 1 ;; *) exit $rc ;; esac
```

## An empty pattern matches everything

The most dangerous single line in a checker. Several tools treat a pattern that
reduces to nothing as matching any input, and the checker then reports the entire
repository as defective. It is loud, so it gets "fixed" quickly — by normalising
everything, after which nothing looks wrong.

```sh
grep -q "$maybe_empty" file     # maybe_empty is unset or stripped
```

Same failure with an empty regex, an empty glob, an empty alternation
`a||b`, and a variable that was set but empty by an earlier branch.

**Assert on an empty input explicitly.** A checker that has never been handed an
empty input has not been tested.

## A cached result is not a check

Anything that short-circuits a check turns it into a decoration:

- a marker file saying "already verified" that nothing invalidates
- a content hash keyed on the wrong inputs, so the check is skipped when it matters
- a result stored under a key that omits the thing being checked
- `--quiet` run in a path that then reports "passing" without the run
- an `|| true` added "just for CI" that outlived its reason

If the check does not execute on the change, the gate has no content.

## Put the check where the change happens

A check that only runs on `main`, or only in a nightly job, or only when someone
remembers, does not gate anything. It **reports**. The difference is whether a bad
change can reach consumers before the check sees it.

| Where it runs | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| Every commit on the branch | a gate |
| Every merge to main | a gate, a late one |
| Nightly | a report |
| On demand | a tool |

Prefer late and reliable over early and flaky: a check developers disable after a
false positive has provided no protection and has cost you the protection it was
supposed to provide.

## False positives are not a cosmetic problem

A check that cries wolf gets muted, and muting it removes it. The cost of a false
positive is therefore not the noise — it is that the next true positive arrives
into a check nobody is reading any more.

Budget for them deliberately: narrow the rule, stage it as a warning, or make the
message say exactly which file and which construct. A check that reports one file
rather than "the codebase" gets fixed. One that reports "several issues" gets
ignored.

## Make the failure explain the fix

The person hitting this gate has one context you do not: what they were trying to
do. A failure that names the file, the construct and the change that resolves it
costs one edit. A failure that says "validation failed" costs a bisect through
your own tooling.

```
FAIL  scripts/normalize.sh:48
      has_cr matches nothing: grep consumes the trailing CR as the pattern's
      own terminator, so use grep -qU instead
```

Path, line, the construct, and the specific replacement. Every part of that is
something the reader would otherwise have to work out alone.

Note what is **absent**: no stack trace, no "unexpected state", no list of every
rule that ran. A message that could apply to any failure applies to no failure.

## Anti-rationalisations

| Excuse | Reality |
| --- | --- |
| "It returned no findings." | That is also what not looking returns. Ask what it scanned. |
| "The test covers it." | A test on clean input passes whether or not the check works. Plant a defect. |
| "It ran green for months." | A broken check is green forever. Duration is not evidence. |
| "It is only a convenience script." | Then a false failure is harmless, so it should be allowed to fail. |
| "I verified the regex once." | Verify it against an input that must not match, and against an empty one. |
| "CI would have caught it." | If it runs only nightly, it is a report. Say which. |
| "False positives are tolerable." | They are what gets the check muted, and muting removes it. |
| "It exits zero." | Check what ran before it, and what the pipeline did to its status. |
